\subsection{Time and persistent lower bounds}
Actual time $t\ge0$ is nondecreasing. Each active gateway and the collector has a nonnegative clock satisfying
\begin{equation}
 |C_i(t)-t|\le\epsilon,\qquad C_i(t)\ge0.
 \label{eq:clock}
\end{equation}
The fixed error bound remains valid after recovery. The argument concerns integer logical times; it does not supply a clock synchronization service. As in systems that expose bounded physical-time uncertainty~\cite{spanner}, the guarantee depends on the bound being sound. A recovering machine whose time bound is unavailable must stop authorizing; durable storage alone does not restore time soundness.

\section{Relationship to Prior Work}
\label{sec:related}
\subsection{The closest revocation and delegation mechanisms}
Offline Delegation addresses transfer of authority without available authorization infrastructure~\cite{offlinedelegation}. Maat combines extended capabilities, secure delegation, and short-lived automatic revocation for parallel file systems~\cite{maat}. The Transform architecture supports distributed capability revocation~\cite{transformcap}. These precedents rule out presenting offline delegation, attenuation, or capability expiry as the contribution here.

